(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-1-physiology-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"Physiology Comprehensive Master Exam — Form D","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"How can the potassium channel be permeable to potassium but not to sodium, even though the hydrated sodium ion is smaller than expected?","choices":["Sodium ions are actively pumped out by a separate ATP-dependent exclusion pump located directly adjacent to the potassium channel's pore, before they ever reach the selectivity filter","Carbonyl oxygens in the selectivity filter strip water molecules from potassium (but not sodium), so only the dehydrated potassium ion (which is actually smaller than dehydrated... larger than hydrated) can permeate","Potassium channels are physically blocked to sodium by a rigid protein plug that only the potassium ion is small enough to displace during channel gating","Sodium carries no net charge once dissolved in physiological solution, and therefore cannot pass through any gated or ungated ion channel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Carbonyl oxygens lining the potassium channel selectivity filter strip water molecules from the potassium ion (but not sodium), allowing the dehydrated potassium ion to permeate while the differently-hydrated sodium ion cannot. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What allows the sodium channel to selectively conduct sodium over the physically larger (hydrated) potassium ion?","choices":["Potassium is repelled by a ring of positively charged lysine residues lining the channel pore, unlike sodium, which is instead attracted inward","Sodium channels have no true selectivity mechanism at all, allowing any similarly-charged cation to pass through the pore freely","Negatively charged amino acids (glutamate) lining the channel pull the sodium ion away from its water shell, allowing the smaller unhydrated sodium ion to diffuse through, while unhydrated potassium is too large","Sodium channels are only open transiently during the mitotic phase of the cell cycle, remaining closed at all other times"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sodium channels are lined with negatively charged amino acids (glutamate) that pull the sodium ion away from its water shell, letting the smaller unhydrated sodium ion diffuse through, while the unhydrated potassium ion is too large to pass. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What limits the maximum rate (Vmax) of facilitated diffusion for a given protein transporter?","choices":["The temperature of the surrounding cytosol only, independent of transporter number","The maximum rate of conformational change of the protein transporter","The concentration of ATP available to power the transporter directly","The total number of ribosomes actively synthesizing new transporter proteins"],"answer":1,"exp":"The maximum rate of facilitated (carrier-mediated) diffusion for a given protein transporter is limited by the maximum rate of conformational change of that transporter, not simply by the number of carriers present. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"How does a hydrostatic pressure difference affect net diffusion across a membrane?","choices":["Pressure has no measurable effect on diffusion rate across any biological membrane whatsoever","Higher pressure results in increased energy available to cause net movement from high to low pressure","Higher pressure always stops net diffusion entirely, regardless of the concentration gradient present","Pressure only affects active, ATP-dependent transport, never passive diffusion across the membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"A hydrostatic pressure difference is one factor affecting net diffusion rate — higher pressure results in increased energy available to cause net movement from the high-pressure side to the low-pressure side. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What is the difference between primary and secondary active transport?","choices":["Both primary and secondary active transport directly hydrolyze ATP molecules to pump their respective solutes across the membrane","Primary active transport only moves potassium ions across the membrane, never sodium, calcium, or hydrogen ions","Secondary active transport requires no energy at all, since it simply moves solutes freely down their own independent gradient","Primary active transport directly uses ATP energy to pump molecules against a gradient; secondary active transport indirectly uses the energy stored in another molecule's electrochemical gradient (usually Na+)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Primary active transport directly uses ATP energy to pump molecules against an electrochemical gradient, while secondary active transport indirectly uses energy stored in the electrochemical gradient of another molecule (usually Na+). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What does the Na+-K+ ATPase pump do, and what happens if it is inhibited (e.g., by ouabain)?","choices":["It has no role in maintaining cellular osmotic balance, functioning instead only to generate nerve signals","It pumps sodium out of cells and potassium into cells against their gradients; inhibition (e.g., by ouabain) causes the cell to swell and burst","It only functions within neurons and glial cells, playing no role whatsoever in other cell types","It pumps sodium into cells and potassium out against their gradients; inhibition causes the cell to shrink"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Na+-K+ ATPase pumps sodium out of cells and potassium into cells against their electrochemical gradients, playing a critical role in osmotic balance — inhibiting the pump (e.g., with ouabain) causes the cell to swell and burst. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is the transport stoichiometry of the Na+-K+ ATPase, and how electrogenic is it?","choices":["3 Na+ out for 2 K+ in; electrogenic, but contributes less than 10% to the membrane potential","1 Na+ out for 1 K+ in; completely non-electrogenic, contributing 0% to the membrane potential","4 Na+ out for 4 K+ in; non-electrogenic, but contributes 50% of the resting membrane potential","2 Na+ out for 3 K+ in; highly electrogenic, contributing most of the membrane potential"],"answer":0,"exp":"The Na+-K+ ATPase transports 3 Na+ out for every 2 K+ in, making it electrogenic — but this electrogenic nature contributes less than 10% to the resting membrane potential. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Where is H+ ATPase found, and what is one of its functions?","choices":["In parietal cells of gastric glands (HCl secretion) and intercalated cells of renal tubules (controlling blood pH), concentrating H+ ions up to a million-fold","In red blood cells only, regulating hemoglobin's oxygen-binding affinity directly during transport","Only in mitochondria, driving ATP synthesis via the electron transport chain's inner membrane proton gradient","Only in the nucleus, regulating chromatin condensation through localized pH changes near DNA"],"answer":0,"exp":"H+ ATPase is found in parietal cells of gastric glands (secreting HCl) and intercalated cells of renal tubules (controlling blood pH), and can concentrate H+ ions up to a million-fold. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is a symporter, in the context of secondary active transport?","choices":["A transporter that moves a substance in the opposite direction of a driver ion","A transporter that moves a substance in the same direction as a \"driver\" ion like Na+","A channel that requires no driver ion, moving substances passively down their own gradient","A pump that directly hydrolyzes ATP to move a substance against its own gradient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Symporters transport a substance in the same direction as a \"driver\" ion like Na+, while antiporters transport a substance in the opposite direction of the driver ion — both are protein cotransporters using an electrochemical gradient (usually sodium's). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What is osmosis?","choices":["The net movement of water across a semipermeable membrane, from an area of high water concentration to an area of low water concentration","The semiconservative replication of DNA prior to cell division during the S phase of the cell cycle","The sequential movement and modification of proteins through the compartments of the Golgi apparatus","The ATP-dependent active transport of ions against their own concentration gradient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Osmosis is the net movement of water across a semipermeable membrane, moving from an area of high water concentration to an area of low water concentration. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"How is osmotic pressure defined?","choices":["The pressure exerted only by dissolved plasma proteins circulating within the vascular compartment","The pressure that must be applied to the solution side to stop fluid movement when a semipermeable membrane separates a solution from pure water","The total hydrostatic pressure measured inside a living cell at any given resting moment","The pressure required to speed up water movement across a membrane, applied to the pure-water side instead"],"answer":1,"exp":"Osmotic pressure is the pressure that must be applied to the solution side to stop fluid movement when a semipermeable membrane separates a solution from pure water, attributed to the solution's osmolarity. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What determines the osmolarity of a solution — the mass of solute, or something else?","choices":["The temperature only, independent of any solute concentration present","The total mass of the solute dissolved, regardless of particle number","The solute particle density (number of particles), not the mass of the solute","The color of the solution, as determined by any dissolved pigments present"],"answer":2,"exp":"The osmolarity of a solution is a function of solute particle density (number of particles per liter), not the mass of the solute — a solution of a low-molecular-weight solute can have far more particles (and thus higher osmolarity) than an equal mass of a high-molecular-weight solute. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What role does tropomyosin play in the thin (actin) filament at rest?","choices":["It binds directly to Ca2+ to initiate contraction, functioning as the primary calcium sensor in place of troponin C","It forms the structural backbone of the thick filament, anchoring myosin heads along its length","It hydrolyzes ATP at the myosin head to power the power stroke of cross-bridge cycling","It covers the active sites on actin, preventing interaction with myosin heads"],"answer":3,"exp":"Tropomyosin covers the active (myosin-binding) sites on the F-actin filament at rest, preventing interaction with myosin heads; troponin (with its I, T, and C subunits, the latter binding Ca2+) regulates this covering. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which troponin subunit binds calcium ions to initiate the contraction process?","choices":["Troponin C (binds Ca2+)","None of the troponin subunits bind calcium","Troponin T (binds tropomyosin)","Troponin I (binds actin)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Troponin C binds Ca2+, while troponin I binds actin and troponin T binds tropomyosin — together the troponin complex regulates the Ca2+-dependent exposure of myosin-binding sites on actin. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Where is the site of ATPase activity located on the myosin molecule?","choices":["In the \"head\" region","On the light chains only","Myosin has no ATPase activity","In the tail region"],"answer":0,"exp":"The myosin molecule (two heavy chains plus four light chains) has its ATPase activity localized to the \"head\" region, which binds actin and undergoes the powerstroke. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What causes myosin heads to detach from actin filaments at the start of a new cross-bridge cycle?","choices":["Release of Pi and ADP from the myosin head","ATP binding to the myosin head","Depolarization of the T-tubule","Calcium leaving the troponin complex"],"answer":1,"exp":"ATP binding to the myosin head causes it to detach from the actin filament; subsequent ATP hydrolysis \"cocks\" the head (with ADP and Pi still bound), and release of Pi and ADP powers the tilting powerstroke. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Why does rigor mortis (muscle stiffening after death) occur?","choices":["Excess ATP floods the sarcoplasm after death, locking the myosin heads in a permanently activated, high-force cross-bridge state","Calcium leaks continuously from the SR after death and can never be resequestered, keeping troponin permanently activated","Troponin degrades completely after death, exposing actin's myosin-binding sites permanently and irreversibly","Without ATP, myosin heads cannot detach from actin filaments, leaving the muscle in a fixed state of contracture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Rigor mortis occurs because, without ATP production after death, myosin heads cannot detach from actin filaments (ATP binding is required for detachment), leaving the muscle in a fixed state of contracture. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"How is active tension (AT) calculated from measurable quantities in the length-tension relationship?","choices":["AT cannot be measured or calculated by any direct or indirect method","AT = TT + PT (total tension plus passive tension combined)","AT = TT - PT (total tension minus passive tension)","AT = TT alone (total tension, with passive tension ignored)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Because active tension cannot be measured directly, it is calculated as active tension (AT) = total tension (TT) - passive tension (PT), where passive tension is the tension required to stretch a resting muscle and total tension is active plus passive tension combined. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"At what sarcomere length is maximal active stress developed in skeletal muscle, and how does cardiac muscle typically differ?","choices":["Cardiac muscle always operates above the optimal ~2 micron length found in skeletal muscle, well past the point of maximal thick-thin filament overlap","Maximal stress occurs only at maximally shortened sarcomere lengths, where thick and thin filaments overlap completely with no remaining gap between them","Sarcomere length has no measurable effect on stress generation, since active tension depends only on the frequency of stimulation","Maximal stress occurs at ~2 microns resting length in skeletal muscle; cardiac muscle normally operates below this optimal length"],"answer":3,"exp":"In skeletal muscle, maximal active stress develops at the normal resting sarcomere length (~2 microns); stress declines at both longer and shorter lengths. Cardiac muscle normally operates at lengths below this optimal length. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What is the difference between an isotonic and an isometric contraction?","choices":["Isotonic contraction: the muscle shortens; isometric contraction: the muscle does not shorten","They are the same type of contraction","Isometric contractions always generate less force than isotonic","Isometric contraction: the muscle shortens; isotonic contraction: the muscle does not shorten"],"answer":0,"exp":"In an isotonic contraction, the muscle shortens (tension is roughly constant while length changes); in an isometric contraction, the muscle generates force without shortening. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What determines the speed of contraction of a skeletal muscle fiber (fast/white vs slow/red)?","choices":["The number of mitochondria present, with fibers containing more mitochondria always cycling actin-myosin cross-bridges faster than fibers with fewer","The diameter of the T-tubules, with wider T-tubules conducting action potentials into the fiber's core faster and enabling quicker contraction","The amount of titin present, with fibers containing more titin cycling cross-bridges more rapidly due to added elastic recoil","The Vmax of myosin ATPase, with high Vmax fibers cycling cross-bridges rapidly (fast) and low Vmax fibers cycling slowly (slow)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The speed of muscle fiber contraction is determined by the Vmax of myosin ATPase: high-Vmax fibers (fast, white) undergo rapid cross-bridge cycling and rapid shortening, while low-Vmax fibers (slow, red) cycle and shorten more slowly. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which fiber type has high resistance to fatigue, small diameter, high myoglobin content, and high mitochondrial content?","choices":["Neither fiber type contains myoglobin","Type 2 (fast, glycolytic) fibers","Both types are identical in these properties","Type 1 (slow, oxidative) fibers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Type 1 (slow, oxidative) fibers have high resistance to fatigue, small diameter, high myoglobin content, high capillary density, high mitochondrial content, and high oxidative enzyme content — the opposite profile of Type 2 (fast, glycolytic) fibers. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What is a motor unit?","choices":["A group of sarcomeres arranged in series within one myofibril","The junction between two terminal cisternae and one T-tubule","A collection of muscle fibers innervated by a single motor neuron","A single denervated muscle fiber with no motor neuron innervation"],"answer":2,"exp":"A motor unit is a collection of muscle fibers innervated by a single motor neuron; all fibers within one motor unit are the same type (fast or slow). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"How do small motor units (e.g., larynx, extraocular muscles) differ functionally from large motor units (e.g., quadriceps)?","choices":["Small motor units (as few as 10 fibers) provide coarse, imprecise control; large motor units (up to 1000 fibers) provide fine, rapid control","Large motor units always contract faster than small ones, since a greater number of fibers generates a larger, faster-rising net force","Motor unit size has no relationship to control precision, which instead depends entirely on the fiber type composition within the unit","Small motor units (as few as 10 fibers) provide precise, rapid control; large motor units (up to 1000 fibers) provide coarser, slower-reacting control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Small motor units (e.g., larynx, extraocular muscles, as few as 10 fibers/unit) provide precise, rapid control, while large motor units (e.g., quadriceps, up to 1000 fibers/unit) provide coarser, slower-reacting control. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"How do fast pain and slow pain differ in timing and character?","choices":["Slow pain only occurs at the body surface and is never triggered by deep tissue damage in muscle, joint capsules, or visceral organs","Fast pain is felt after 1 second and has a dull, throbbing, aching quality; slow pain is felt within 0.1 second and has a sharp, stabbing quality","Fast and slow pain travel via the exact same nerve fiber type and spinal tract, differing only in the intensity of the triggering stimulus","Fast pain is felt within 0.1 second and is sharp (e.g., knife cut); slow pain is felt after 1 second or more and is throbbing/aching, associated with tissue destruction (bradykinin)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Fast pain (body surface only) is felt within 0.1 seconds and is sharp (like a knife cut or needle poke); slow pain (surface and deep tissues) is felt after 1 second or more and is throbbing/aching, usually associated with tissue destruction and bradykinin release. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Do pain receptors adapt to a sustained stimulus, and what does pain intensity correlate with?","choices":["Pain receptors adapt slowly, over several minutes, reaching about half their initial response before stabilizing","Pain receptors do not adapt to the stimulus; intensity of pain correlates with the rate of tissue damage, not the total amount","Pain intensity correlates with total cumulative tissue damage rather than the rate at which damage occurs","Pain receptors adapt partially within seconds; pain intensity correlates with the total surface area of tissue affected"],"answer":1,"exp":"Pain receptors do not adapt to the stimulus (unlike most other receptors), and the intensity of pain correlates with the rate of tissue damage rather than the total amount of damage. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What is the main chemical mediator of pain resulting from tissue damage (slow pain)?","choices":["Acetylcholine (with contributions from norepinephrine and epinephrine)","Dopamine (with contributions from serotonin and histamine)","Serotonin (with contributions from calcitonin gene-related peptide)","Bradykinin (with contributions from potassium and proteolytic enzymes)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Bradykinin is the main cause of pain resulting from tissue damage (slow pain), with potassium and proteolytic enzymes also contributing. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which fiber type and tract carries fast, sharp pain, and which carries slow, burning pain?","choices":["Fast pain: type A-beta fibers via the dorsal column-medial lemniscal system; slow pain: type C fibers via the anterolateral system","Both fast and slow pain travel via type A-delta fibers through the neospinothalamic tract exclusively","Fast pain: type A-delta fibers via the neospinothalamic tract; slow pain: type C fibers via the paleospinothalamic tract","Fast pain: type C fibers via the paleospinothalamic tract; slow pain: type A-delta fibers via the neospinothalamic tract"],"answer":2,"exp":"Fast, sharp pain is carried by type A-delta fibers (6-30 m/sec) via the neospinothalamic tract; slow, burning pain is carried by type C fibers (0.5-2 m/sec) via the paleospinothalamic tract — explaining the double sensation of pain (sharp pain followed by slow pain). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What neurotransmitter is used by A-delta pain fiber nerve endings entering the neospinothalamic tract, and where do most of these fibers terminate?","choices":["Serotonin; most terminate diffusely in the hypothalamus, providing poor localization of the pain sensation","Substance P; most terminate diffusely in the reticular formation, providing only crude localization of pain","GABA; most terminate in the cerebellum, contributing to motor withdrawal rather than conscious pain perception","Glutamate; most terminate in the ventrobasal complex of the thalamus, allowing good localization of fast, sharp pain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Glutamate is the excitatory transmitter of A-delta pain fiber nerve endings; the second-order neuron crosses immediately and most fibers travel via the anterolateral columns to the ventrobasal complex of the thalamus, allowing fast, sharp pain to be well localized. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What neurotransmitter is used by type C pain fibers in the paleospinothalamic tract, and how well can slow pain be localized?","choices":["GABA; slow pain is well localized to a single point, since virtually all fibers terminate directly in the ventrobasal thalamus before reaching the cortex","Glutamate; slow pain is precisely localized to a single point, since most fibers terminate directly in the ventrobasal complex of the thalamus","Substance P; slow pain is poorly localized, often only to the affected limb or body part, since most fibers terminate diffusely in brainstem reticular nuclei rather than the thalamus","Dopamine; localization is identical to fast pain, since these fibers also terminate primarily in the ventrobasal thalamus rather than the reticular formation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Substance P is the excitatory transmitter of type C pain fiber nerve endings in the paleospinothalamic tract; only 10-25% of these fibers terminate in the thalamus (most terminate diffusely in reticular nuclei of the medulla, pons, and mesencephalon), resulting in poor localization of slow pain. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What over-the-counter treatment can deplete Substance P from local nerve endings to relieve pain?","choices":["Menthol-based creams for temporary cooling relief","Prescription-strength lidocaine patches","Capsaicin creams (made from chili peppers)","Topical ibuprofen or diclofenac gel"],"answer":2,"exp":"Over-the-counter creams containing capsaicin (made from chili peppers) can deplete Substance P from local nerve endings, relieving pain thought to be mediated by Substance P (e.g., lower back pain, arthritis, fibromyalgia). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What role do serotonin (from the Raphe nuclei) and enkephalin play in the brain's endogenous analgesia system?","choices":["Both substances act only on motor neurons in the anterior horn, enhancing alpha motor neuron excitability during spinal withdrawal reflexes to pain","Serotonin from the Raphe nuclei stimulates dorsal column interneurons that secrete enkephalin, an opioid peptide causing pre- and postsynaptic inhibition of incoming C and A-delta pain fibers","Enkephalin increases pain transmission by directly facilitating glutamate release from incoming C and A-delta pain fiber terminals in the dorsal horn","Serotonin has no role in pain modulation at all, acting instead exclusively on mood and sleep circuits originating from the Raphe nuclei"],"answer":1,"exp":"Serotonin released from the Raphe nuclei stimulates interneurons in the dorsal horn that secrete enkephalin, an opioid peptide that causes both presynaptic and postsynaptic inhibition of incoming type C and A-delta pain fibers where they synapse in the dorsal horn. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which endogenous opiate is found in the hypothalamus and pituitary, distinct from enkephalins and dynorphin (found in the brainstem and spinal cord)?","choices":["Substance P","Serotonin","Bradykinin","Beta-endorphin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Beta-endorphin is found in the hypothalamus and pituitary, while enkephalins and dynorphin are found in the brainstem and spinal cord — together these major endogenous opiates suppress pain during stress and emergencies. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"According to the gate theory of pain, how does stimulation of type A-beta tactile fibers reduce pain transmission?","choices":["By increasing bradykinin release at the site of stimulation, which paradoxically desensitizes local nociceptors to further painful input","Via a type of lateral inhibition of pain fibers by mechanosensory fibers, likely the mechanism behind massage and electrical stimulation of skin for pain relief","By directly destroying pain fiber terminals in the skin, a permanent effect exploited by capsaicin-based topical treatments","A-beta fibers only affect proprioception and vibration sense, with no measurable effect on pain perception or dorsal horn transmission"],"answer":1,"exp":"The gate theory of pain holds that stimulation of A-beta fibers from peripheral tactile receptors decreases pain signal transmission via a type of lateral inhibition of pain fibers by mechanosensory fibers — likely the mechanism behind massage, liniments, and electrical stimulation for pain relief. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Where is cardiac (heart) pain classically referred to?","choices":["The lower back","Above the umbilicus only","The right lower quadrant of the abdomen","The neck, left shoulder, and arm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Referred pain is localized to the dermatome of embryological origin; cardiac pain is classically referred to the neck, left shoulder, and arm, while stomach pain is referred above the umbilicus and colon pain below the umbilicus. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What is the proposed mechanism underlying referred pain from visceral organs?","choices":["Intermingling of second-order neurons in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord that receive input from both skin and viscera","Direct anastomoses connect visceral afferent nerve fibers to cutaneous nerve fibers at the level of the dorsal root ganglion","Visceral organs are innervated by the same cutaneous nerve trunks that supply the overlying dermatome directly","Referred pain results from sensitization of peripheral nociceptors within the visceral organ itself, independent of the spinal cord"],"answer":0,"exp":"Referred pain from visceral sources is thought to result from intermingling of second-order neurons in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord, which receive convergent input from both skin and visceral afferents. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What kind of movement does stimulation of the red nucleus (magnocellular portion) produce, compared to the primary motor cortex?","choices":["No movement at all, since the red nucleus's magnocellular portion functions purely as a sensory relay station with no direct motor output of its own","Movement identical in precision to primary motor cortex stimulation, since the rubrospinal tract is thought to carry an exact duplicate of the corticospinal signal","Relatively fine motor movement, but not as discrete as movement from the primary motor cortex — an accessory route for discrete signal transmission","Only coordinated movements of the eyes, since the rubrospinal tract is believed to project exclusively to the extraocular motor nuclei of the brainstem"],"answer":2,"exp":"Stimulation of the red nucleus's magnocellular portion (which receives substantial input from the primary motor cortex and gives rise to the rubrospinal tract) causes relatively fine motor movement, though not as discrete as movement produced by the primary motor cortex — serving as an accessory route for discrete motor signals. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How do the pontine and medullary reticular nuclei differ in their effect on antigravity muscles?","choices":["Neither nucleus affects antigravity muscles directly at all; both are thought to act exclusively through the cerebellum to modulate limb flexor tone during gait","The pontine reticular nuclei excite antigravity muscles; the medullary reticular nuclei inhibit them","The pontine reticular nuclei inhibit antigravity muscles and have low natural excitability; the medullary reticular nuclei excite them and receive collateral input from higher centers","Both nuclei excite antigravity muscles to an equal degree, differing only in the conduction speed of the pontine versus medullary reticulospinal tracts"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pontine reticular nuclei excite antigravity muscles (via the pontine reticulospinal tract) and have high natural excitability; the medullary reticular nuclei inhibit antigravity muscles (via the medullary reticulospinal tract) and receive collateral input from the corticospinal and rubrospinal tracts to counterbalance the pons. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What sensory information does the macula (within the utricle and saccule) detect?","choices":["Angular (rotational) acceleration of the head, detected via the inertial lag of endolymph fluid within the semicircular ducts rather than by gravity","Sound frequency, via mechanically tuned hair cells arranged tonotopically along the basilar membrane rather than within the macula itself","The position of objects within the visual field, via signals relayed from the retina through the vestibular nuclei during head movement","The orientation of the head with respect to gravity, via gravity-sensitive hair cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"The macula, found within the utricle and saccule, is a gravity-sensitive receptor consisting of gravity-sensitive hair cells (made top-heavy by statoconia) that detect the orientation of the head with respect to gravity. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What happens to a vestibular hair cell when its stereocilia bend toward the kinocilium versus away from it?","choices":["Bending in one direction causes depolarization; bending in the opposite direction causes hyperpolarization","Bending of the stereocilia has no electrical effect on hair cells at all; only sustained static pressure changes their resting membrane potential","Hair cells are not directionally sensitive at all, responding with an identical depolarization regardless of which way their stereocilia are bent","Bending in either direction always causes depolarization, with the kinocilium serving only to anchor the bundle rather than set polarity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vestibular hair cells have stereocilia and one large kinocilium that make them directionally sensitive: bending in one direction causes depolarization, while bending in the opposite direction causes hyperpolarization. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What do the semicircular ducts detect, and how are they oriented?","choices":["Only vertical head movements confined to the sagittal plane, since the ducts are oriented in parallel with one another rather than at right angles","Linear acceleration and gravity, sensed by gravity-sensitive hair cells oriented within a single horizontal plane inside each of the three ducts","Sound vibration transmitted through the endolymph, with all three ducts on each side oriented parallel to one another to create a tonotopic frequency map","Angular (rotational) acceleration of the head, with all three ducts oriented at 90 degrees to each other representing the three planes in space"],"answer":3,"exp":"The semicircular ducts detect angular (rotational) acceleration of the head; all three ducts on each side are oriented at 90 degrees to each other, representing all three planes in space, with an ampulla (containing the crista ampullaris) at the end of each duct. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"How does rotation of a semicircular duct activate its sensory receptors?","choices":["The bony duct wall itself directly senses rotation through embedded stretch receptors, entirely without any relative movement of the endolymph fluid inside it","Endolymph tends to remain stationary due to inertia while the duct rotates around it, causing relative movement of endolymph that activates receptors in the crista ampullaris","Rotation causes the duct itself to physically shrink and compress the crista ampullaris, directly triggering receptor activation without fluid involvement","Perilymph, not endolymph, provides the sensory signal by lagging behind the rotating duct wall and displacing the crista ampullaris"],"answer":1,"exp":"Because endolymph tends to remain stationary due to inertia, rotation of the duct in one direction causes relative movement of the endolymph in the opposite direction, activating receptors in the crista ampullaris; stopping rotation produces the opposite effect. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is the \"predictive\" function of the semicircular ducts in maintaining equilibrium?","choices":["They predict situations in which equilibrium will be affected, allowing corrective measures to be initiated before equilibrium is actually lost","They have no predictive function at all, serving only to passively confirm equilibrium loss after it has already occurred, well after corrective reflexes begin","They only respond after equilibrium has already been fully lost, sending corrective signals to the brain only once an actual fall has already begun","They only function during sleep, becoming active specifically when neck proprioceptors and visual input are unavailable to help maintain equilibrium"],"answer":0,"exp":"The semicircular ducts predict situations in which equilibrium will be affected, sending this information to the brain so that corrective measures can be initiated before equilibrium is actually affected; neck proprioceptors and visual input also contribute. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is the general function of the cerebellum in motor control?","choices":["It only functions during periods of sleep, generating the rhythmic motor patterns responsible for eye movements and muscle twitches seen during REM","It initiates all voluntary movement entirely independently, with the motor cortex serving only a minor supporting role once a movement has been planned there","It has no role in movement coordination whatsoever, functioning instead purely as a site for long-term procedural memory storage and consolidation","It coordinates and sequences muscle activity, monitoring and making corrective adjustments by comparing actual motor movements with intended movements"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cerebellum (\"little brain\") coordinates muscle activity, sequences motor activities, and monitors/makes corrective adjustments to movements initiated by other parts of the brain by comparing actual motor movements with intended movements. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which functional zone of the cerebellum is responsible for the sequencing, timing, and coordination of movement?","choices":["None of these zones is involved in timing","The lateral zone","The intermediate zone","The vermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cerebellum is functionally organized along its longitudinal axis: the vermis (center) controls axial movements of neck/shoulders/hips, the intermediate zone controls motion of distal limbs (especially hands/feet), and the lateral zone controls sequencing, timing, and coordination of movement. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What pattern of signaling do deep cerebellar nuclear cells produce at the beginning of a motion, and what function does this serve?","choices":["Only continuous inhibitory signals throughout the entire motion, suppressing the movement steadily from initiation until the intended target is finally reached","Excitatory signals to enhance movement, followed milliseconds later by inhibitory signals, providing a damping function to prevent overshoot (like a delay-line circuit)","Only excitatory signals with no damping component, continuing to accelerate the movement throughout its full course with no braking mechanism at all","Random, unpatterned signals bearing no consistent timing relationship to the onset, progression, or completion of the intended motion"],"answer":1,"exp":"At the beginning of motion, deep cerebellar nuclear cells send excitatory signals into the motor pathway to enhance movement, followed milliseconds later by inhibitory signals — providing a damping function that prevents the movement from overshooting its mark, resembling a delay-line negative feedback circuit. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What role does climbing fiber input play in Purkinje cell function?","choices":["It has no lasting effect on Purkinje cells at all, producing only a brief, immediately reversible depolarization with no change to their future sensitivity","It only affects the inferior olivary complex itself, feeding back locally without ever reaching or altering Purkinje cell activity in the cerebellar cortex","It adjusts the long-term sensitivity of Purkinje cells to mossy fiber input (from muscle spindle, Golgi tendon organ, proprioceptors), adjusting the feedback control of muscle movement","It directly innervates skeletal muscle fibers itself, bypassing Purkinje cells and the deep cerebellar nuclei entirely to produce contraction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Climbing fiber input adjusts the sensitivity of Purkinje cells to stimulation by parallel fibers, changing the long-term sensitivity of Purkinje cells to mossy fiber input (from muscle spindle, Golgi tendon organ, and proprioceptors) — adjusting the feedback control of muscle movement, since precise motor movement must be learned. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What is the function of the inferior olivary complex in correcting motor errors?","choices":["It compares the intent of movement (from the corticospinal tract and brainstem motor centers) with the actual movement that occurred (via sensory feedback), altering climbing fiber output to the cerebellum to correct any mismatch","It has no role in motor error correction at all, functioning instead purely as a relay station for auditory input on its way to the thalamus","It only processes visual information, comparing retinal input with expected visual flow patterns to help stabilize gaze during head movement","It directly controls the basal ganglia with no cerebellar involvement at all, sending its motor error signals straight to the striatum instead"],"answer":0,"exp":"The inferior olivary complex receives input from the corticospinal tract and brainstem motor centers (representing intended movement) as well as sensory information about actual movement; if a mismatch occurs, its output to the cerebellum via climbing fibers is altered to correct the mismatch. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What is the functional division of labor between Wernicke's area and Broca's area in speech?","choices":["Both areas perform identical, fully redundant functions, each independently forming thoughts and coordinating the muscles of speech","Wernicke's area is responsible for the formation of thoughts and choice of words; Broca's area controls the motor coordination required for speech","Wernicke's area controls the motor coordination of speech muscles; Broca's area forms thoughts and chooses the words to be spoken","Neither area is actually involved in speech at all, with both functions instead localized entirely to the cerebellum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Wernicke's area is responsible for the formation of thoughts to be expressed and the choice of words, while Broca's area controls the motor coordination required for actually speaking those words. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What can result from interruption of the corpus callosum fibers connecting the two hemispheres?","choices":["Bizarre anomalies, such as the nondominant hemisphere eliciting a motor response without the dominant hemisphere (which understands spoken word) knowing why it occurred","Complete and immediate loss of vision in both eyes simultaneously and permanently, with no ability to perceive light, shape, or motion of any kind","Immediate and total loss of consciousness lasting several minutes, followed by a prolonged period of confusion and disorientation upon waking","No effect at all, since the two cerebral hemispheres function completely independently and have no need to share information via connecting fibers"],"answer":0,"exp":"The corpus callosum connects the two hemispheres and allows transfer of information; interruption of these fibers can lead to bizarre anomalies, such as the nondominant hemisphere (which understands written word) eliciting a motor response without the dominant hemisphere (which understands spoken word) knowing why the response was performed. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What are the three types of memory classified by duration?","choices":["Explicit memory (conscious recall of facts, lasting days to years) and implicit memory (unconscious skill memory, lasting a lifetime)","Sensory memory (milliseconds to seconds), working memory (seconds to minutes), and procedural memory (a lifetime)","Immediate memory (seconds to minutes), short-term memory (days to weeks), and long-term memory (years to a lifetime)","Only two types exist by duration: short-term memory (minutes to hours) and long-term memory (years to a lifetime)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Memory is classified into three types by duration: immediate memory (lasts seconds to minutes), short-term memory (lasts days to weeks), and long-term memory (lasts years or a lifetime). — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the proposed mechanism of immediate memory?","choices":["A complete anatomical rewiring of neural circuits, in which new axon collaterals sprout to form entirely new synaptic connections","A structural increase in synapse size through synthesis of new release-site proteins, enlarging the vesicular release area","Synaptic potentiation through accumulation of calcium in the presynaptic membrane, promoting neurotransmitter release","A progressive loss of active calcium channels at the presynaptic terminal, reducing neurotransmitter release"],"answer":2,"exp":"Immediate memory may result from synaptic potentiation through the accumulation of calcium in the presynaptic membrane, which promotes neurotransmitter release; short-term memory may result from a temporary physical or chemical change in the pre- or postsynaptic membrane. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is habituation, and what is its cellular mechanism?","choices":["A progressive decline in response sensitivity to repetitive stimulation, resulting from a progressive decline in the number of active calcium channels (less calcium entry, less transmitter released)","Sensitization -- an increased response to repetitive or noxious stimulation, resulting from facilitation of transmitter release via increased calcium influx at the presynaptic terminal","Synaptic potentiation from calcium accumulation in the presynaptic membrane, a process that strengthens rather than weakens neurotransmitter release with repeated stimulation","A structural change in synapse size due to new release-site protein synthesis, permanently increasing the area available for vesicular transmitter release"],"answer":0,"exp":"Habituation is a progressive decline in sensitivity/response resulting from repetitive stimulation, caused by a progressive decline in the number of active calcium channels at the presynaptic terminal — less calcium entry means less neurotransmitter released; it reflects the nervous system learning that a stimulus is unimportant. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What structural change underlies long-term memory formation?","choices":["A temporary chemical or physical change in the pre- or postsynaptic membrane, lasting only minutes before reversing back to baseline","A progressive decline in the number of active calcium channels at the presynaptic terminal, reducing transmitter release with each stimulation","A structural change in the synapse — an increase in the area for vesicular release (from synthesis of release site proteins), so more transmitter is released","A decrease in the area available for vesicular release, caused by degradation of release site proteins during inactivity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Long-term memory results from a structural change in the synapse: an increase in the area for vesicular release (from synthesis of release site proteins) means more transmitter is released; during periods of inactivity, this area decreases in size. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What can interrupt the consolidation of memory (converting immediate memory into short- and long-term memory)?","choices":["Deep sleep, which always interrupts consolidation since neural activity is minimized during that stage","Rehearsal, which interrupts consolidation by overloading the immediate memory circuit with competing information","Only direct physical injury to the hippocampus can interrupt it, since consolidation occurs there exclusively","Electrical shock or anesthesia, which can prevent memory development since consolidation requires time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Memory consolidation (converting immediate into short- and long-term memory, via chemical/physical/anatomical synaptic changes) requires time and can be interrupted by electrical shock or anesthesia, preventing memory development; rehearsal, by contrast, enhances consolidation. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What deficit results from hippocampal damage, and what is this condition called?","choices":["Complete loss of all spinal and brainstem reflexes, including the patellar and pupillary reflexes","Anterograde amnesia — inability to form new verbal or symbolic long-term memory","Loss of motor speech production (Broca aphasia) with intact language comprehension","Retrograde amnesia -- inability to recall memories formed before the damage occurred"],"answer":1,"exp":"The hippocampus is critical for long-term memory; damage causes anterograde amnesia — the inability to form new verbal or symbolic long-term memories — and the hippocampus also helps determine which sensory experiences are important enough to warrant attention. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What deficit results from thalamic damage related to memory, and what is this condition called?","choices":["Retrograde amnesia — the inability to recall previously stored experiences, since the thalamus scans the cortex for the stored memory's location/circuit","Only a mild deficit in sensory relay occurs, since the thalamus plays no direct role in memory storage or retrieval","A selective loss of procedural memory only, while declarative memory recall remains fully intact","Anterograde amnesia, identical to the deficit caused by hippocampal damage, since both structures scan the cortex together"],"answer":0,"exp":"Thalamic structures are important for recalling memories; damage to the thalamus causes retrograde amnesia, the inability to recall previously stored experiences, since the thalamus scans the cortex for the area and circuit holding the stored memory. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the function of the bulboreticular facilitatory area of the brainstem?","choices":["It sends purely inhibitory signals to antigravity muscles and receives input only from the cerebellum, bypassing the thalamus entirely","It sends inhibitory signals to the reticular inhibitory area and to spinal flexor circuits, and is itself excited by input from the basal ganglia","It sends excitatory signals to antigravity muscles and to the thalamus (distributed to widespread cortical areas), and is itself excited by peripheral pain signals and cortical feedback","It exclusively governs circadian rhythm timing through direct projections to the suprachiasmatic nucleus and hypothalamic sleep centers"],"answer":2,"exp":"The bulboreticular facilitatory area sends excitatory signals to the antigravity muscles and to the thalamus (from which they distribute to widespread cortical areas); it is excited by peripheral signals (especially pain) and by positive feedback from the cortex itself. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the function of the reticular inhibitory area of the brainstem?","choices":["It only affects the spinal reflex arcs, suppressing flexor withdrawal reflexes without any influence on the cortex","It sends excitatory signals directly to the cortex, bypassing the bulboreticular facilitatory area and the thalamus entirely","It has no direct effect on cortical activity, acting instead solely to dampen spinal motor neuron excitability","It sends inhibitory signals to the bulboreticular facilitatory area, decreasing cortical activity when excited"],"answer":3,"exp":"The reticular inhibitory area sends inhibitory signals to the bulboreticular facilitatory area; when the inhibitory area is excited, it decreases the activity of the excitatory area and thus decreases overall cortical activity. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the limbic system, and what functions does it govern?","choices":["A division of the peripheral autonomic nervous system responsible for regulating glandular secretion and smooth muscle tone","A single, discrete nucleus located within the cerebellar vermis, involved solely in coordinating fine motor timing","A network of interconnected brain structures beneath the cerebral cortex, around the thalamus, central to emotion, motivation, memory, and survival-related behaviors","A collection of white matter tracts exclusively involved in relaying voluntary motor commands from the cortex to the spinal cord"],"answer":2,"exp":"The limbic system is a network of interconnected brain structures beneath the cerebral cortex and around the thalamus, forming a functional hub central to generating and regulating emotional responses (fear, pleasure, anger, anxiety), memory consolidation, reward-seeking behavior, and survival instincts. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 57"}]};
